Requirement 8.3 – Multi-Environment Deployment Readiness Dashboard

Objective

The Deployment Readiness section should provide a clear overview of deployment status across all mandatory deployment environments used during the release process. The goal is to allow users to quickly identify which environments are configured correctly, which Kubernetes connections are available, and the deployment readiness for each environment.

The UI should be redesigned from a dashboard perspective rather than showing a single environment at a time.

⸻

Standard Deployment Environments

The application must support the following deployment environments:

Environment	Namespace	Kubernetes Cookie
Test-3	com-att-oc-fmo-test-3	Test Cookie
Test-4	com-att-oc-fmo-test-4	Test Cookie
Stage-West	com-att-oc-fmo	Stage-West Cookie
Production-East	com-att-oc-fmo	Production-East Cookie
Production-West	com-att-oc-fmo	Production-West Cookie

These five environments represent the standard release promotion path and should be considered first-class deployment targets throughout the application.

⸻

Requirement 8.4 – Deployment Readiness Dashboard

Instead of requiring the user to repeatedly change environments and rerun validation, redesign this section as a deployment readiness dashboard.

The dashboard should display all five environments together in a clean, compact layout.

Each environment should display:

* Environment name
* Namespace
* Connection status
* Cookie availability
* Last refresh time
* Deployment readiness summary
* Number of validated components
* Number of pending components
* Number of failed validations (if any)

The UI should allow users to immediately identify which environment requires attention without navigating through multiple screens.

⸻

Requirement 8.5 – Kubernetes Connection Status

Each environment should clearly display its Kubernetes connection status.

Example statuses include:

* Connected
* Cookie Available
* Cookie Missing
* Connection Test Failed
* Last Successfully Connected
* Refresh In Progress

Use clear visual indicators (success, warning, error, loading) consistent with the application’s existing AT&T theme.

⸻

Requirement 8.6 – Environment Selection Experience

The developer may redesign this section using the most appropriate UX pattern.

Possible implementations include:

* Dashboard cards
* Environment tabs
* Accordion sections
* Responsive grid
* Expandable panels

The exact UI implementation is left to the developer’s UX expertise.

The primary objective is that users can quickly switch between environments while keeping the interface clean and intuitive.

⸻

Requirement 8.7 – Intelligent Namespace Resolution

The system should automatically determine the appropriate Kubernetes cluster and authentication based on the selected environment.

Users should never need to manually understand cluster mappings.

Internally:

* Test-3 and Test-4 use the Test Kubernetes cluster and Test Cookie.
* Stage-West uses the Stage-West cluster and Stage-West Cookie.
* Production-East uses the Production-East cluster and Production-East Cookie.
* Production-West uses the Production-West cluster and Production-West Cookie.

This mapping should be maintained centrally and reused throughout the application.

⸻

Requirement 8.8 – Reuse Existing Cached Deployment Data

Where deployment readiness information has already been collected and stored in the database, reuse that data instead of making additional Kubernetes API calls.

Only perform a Kubernetes refresh when the user explicitly requests a refresh or when cached data is unavailable.

This minimizes external API calls and improves overall application performance.

⸻

Acceptance Criteria

1. The Deployment Readiness page must support all five standard deployment environments.
2. The UI must present deployment readiness in a clean dashboard-style layout.
3. Users should be able to identify connection status for every environment at a glance.
4. Namespace and cookie mapping must happen automatically based on the selected environment.
5. Existing deployment validation logic must remain unchanged.
6. Existing Kubernetes integration must remain unchanged.
7. Existing database caching must be reused wherever possible.
8. Unnecessary Kubernetes API calls must be avoided.
9. The redesigned UI must follow the overall AT&T blue/white design language and remain responsive across different screen sizes.
10. No existing deployment readiness functionality should regress as part of this enhancement.
